<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Deliberately minimal accessibility service configuration.

    accessibilityEventTypes="typeViewTextChanged|typeViewLongClicked"
        The ONLY two event types this service subscribes to, as of v1.2.10.

        typeViewTextChanged powers the typed "?en"/"?pl" trigger pathway:
        "the text in the field currently being edited changed."

        typeViewLongClicked powers the separate long-press "translate what's
        under my finger" bubble pathway, added later: "the user long-pressed
        a view." This is the standard, purpose-built accessibility signal
        for a long-press and requires no additional capability flag to
        receive — in particular it does NOT require
        flagRequestTouchExplorationMode, which would turn the whole device
        into TalkBack-style screen-reader navigation and was deliberately
        rejected as a way to detect this gesture. One direct consequence of
        using this event instead of raw touch tracking: this service cannot
        observe exactly when the user's finger lifts off the screen, only
        that a long-press happened — which is why the resulting translation
        bubble (see TranslationBubble.kt) dismisses itself via an explicit
        close button, a tap outside the bubble, or an auto-dismiss timer,
        rather than "on finger release."

        A short history of the OTHER event types tried and removed, in
        full in README.md §14 ("Amendment to Feature update #6" and every
        one after it) — summarized here so this file stays honest about
        what actually shipped and why, without repeating the whole story:

        typeViewSelected (added v1.2.4, REMOVED v1.2.10) was tried as a
        long-press fallback for apps (confirmed: Google Messages) that
        never dispatch typeViewLongClicked at all. It never once worked
        for that purpose — real-device testing repeatedly showed it also
        never fires for a genuine long-press in Google Messages or X. It
        was kept subscribed anyway on the theory that a standard,
        zero-cost signal "might help other apps." v1.2.10 removed it for
        good after on-device testing showed the opposite: it fires for
        ordinary, non-long-press taps on tab/toggle-style buttons in other
        Compose-based apps (confirmed: TikTok's navigation), because
        Compose legitimately dispatches typeViewSelected for real Tab-role
        selection changes — a completely different, unrelated situation
        this service had no way to distinguish from "long-press to
        multi-select." handleLongClick() treated both the same way, so
        this caused REAL, unwanted BubbleTranslateGate translation
        requests — actual Gemini calls and a real translation bubble — on
        ordinary taps. See README.md §14 "Seventh amendment" for the full
        writeup. Zero working translations gained, one real bug caused:
        removing it is a clear net improvement with no known downside.

        typeWindowContentChanged (added v1.2.5, removed v1.2.6; added
        again v1.2.7, removed again v1.2.9) was tried twice as a
        diagnostic-only signal to investigate whether Compose still
        announces the RESULTING UI change from a long-press even though it
        never announces the gesture itself. Both times it was confirmed to
        fire, and both times it was found too generic/inconclusive to
        justify keeping (see README.md §14 "Third", "Fourth", "Fifth", and
        "Sixth amendment" for the two full attempts, including two claims
        that WERE confirmed directly against the real AndroidX Compose
        source before this was dropped for the second time).

        This service does NOT receive typeViewFocused, typeWindowStateChanged,
        typeWindowContentChanged, typeViewSelected, typeViewClicked (a
        normal, non-long tap), or any other event type — it only ever
        observes "a field's text changed" and "a view was long-pressed,"
        nothing about navigation, scrolling, or which screens the user
        visits.

    We deliberately do NOT set:
      - android:flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows — this service never enumerates
        or inspects any window other than the one that raised the event.
      - android:flagIncludeNotImportantViews — decorative/non-input views are
        never read.
      - android:packageNames — left unset (all packages) at the OS
        subscription level ONLY because the OS accessibility API has no
        concept of a *dynamically user-editable* allow-list here; the real
        enforcement happens first thing in
        TextGateAccessibilityService.onAccessibilityEvent(), which checks
        event.packageName against the user's saved allow-list and the
        built-in block-list and returns immediately for anything not
        explicitly allowed, before any further processing. This is true for
        BOTH event types: the long-press bubble pathway is gated by
        BubbleTranslateGate, which enforces the exact same block-list and
        allow-list as the typed-trigger pathway (see BubbleTranslateGate.kt).

    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true" is required for
    AccessibilityEvent#getSource() to return a usable node at all; without
    it the service could not read the field being edited, nor the
    long-pressed message content, even after the user grants permission. This is intentionally the ONLY window-content capability
    granted — the service never calls AccessibilityService#getWindows() or
    #getRootInActiveWindow(), and never enumerates any window other than
    the one that raised the event; it only ever inspects event.getSource()
    and — for the long-press bubble pathway ONLY, when that node's own
    text is blank — that SAME node's own descendants (never siblings,
    never ancestors, bounded search; see BubbleTranslateGate.kt's
    "Where the text actually lives" doc section for the full reasoning).
    The typed-trigger pathway (EventGate) still only ever reads
    event.getSource() directly, with no further traversal of any kind.

    android:notificationTimeout="0" — events are delivered immediately, not
    batched, so no accessibility event is ever buffered/queued by the
    framework on our behalf.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeViewTextChanged|typeViewLongClicked"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags=""
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"
    android:notificationTimeout="0"
    android:description="@string/accessibility_service_description"
    android:settingsActivity="com.textgate.ai.settings.SettingsActivity" />
